{
  "adaptations": [
    {
      "file": "src/crosswords/grid/resize.rs",
      "reason": "MIXVIU LOCAL PATCH (#106): deliberate correction of pinned Gold/upstream shrink reflow. Consume trailing newest blank viewport rows before adding history, bounded by live viewport expansion, never history expansion. Storage/take_all are newest-first; the reversed walk builds oldest-first and only then truncates blank rows at its newest tail. Debug assertions pin complete viewport storage and blank-only truncation. Correct the two reversal comments to newest-first storage order. Preserve cursor displacement and mark dropped remap rows with the established -1 sentinel. Retain the labelled hunks on refresh until an upstream fix passes the terminal conformance matrix (history plus consumption, already-wrapped lines, CJK, scroll margins, alternate screen and simultaneous height changes)."
    },
    {
      "file": "Cargo.toml",
      "reason": "Materialise upstream workspace package/dependency inheritance; all sibling Rio crates retain the same pinned git source. Preserve features and dependency flags."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/crosswords/mod.rs",
      "reason": "Fable C1 functional Gold defect: ED1 from second row must erase first row; guard cursor.row > 1 becomes > 0. Existing Viu ED assertions retained."
    },
    {
      "file": "LICENSE",
      "reason": "Copy upstream root MIT licence alongside this extracted crate."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/crosswords/grid/resize.rs",
      "reason": "Fable C1 functional Gold defect: grow_columns calculates temporary cursor movement directly using the new width before relocating content. Avoid viewport clamping and avoid position helpers consulting temporarily taken grid storage. Native Gold/unmodified port hide the rejoined line and lose cursor column after narrow/wide. Exact text/selection/final-cursor assertions and a width/cursor matrix are retained."
    }
  ],
  "exit_path": "Upstream ED1 and cursor reflow corrections, replace local patches with a fixed reviewed Rio revision, rerun conformance and native parity.",
